Subject: Host other profiles' Spaces in the same window

KryptonUI Spaces may each use their own persistent profile, and several Spaces
may share one. A Space of another profile is shown by a real Browser of that
profile, so its tabs, popups, permissions, saved credentials, extensions,
autocomplete, history and sessions keep Chromium's own per-profile ownership.
Its BrowserWidget becomes a child widget of the window's frame (one HWND and
compositor) instead of a top-level window; the frame presents exactly one
profile at a time.

- BrowserNativeWidgetFactory asks KryptonUI for a hosted native widget first.
  It returns null for every ordinary browser window.
- BrowserView::OnWindowCloseRequested lets a frame close each hosted profile
  through that profile's own beforeunload handling before closing itself.
- Session rebuilding re-emits the window identity and host relationship as
  window extra data, and session restore exposes a restored window's extra
  data while it creates that window, so a restored host finds its frame.

Nothing copies, migrates or substitutes profile data. All changes are gated by
ENABLE_KRYPTON_UI; the owned implementation lives in chromium_src.

Inactive profile Views, browser UI anchors and local extension shortcuts follow the presented host.

